free the draw from cpu 0

0045 kept the draw to cpu 0 and the emulator to cpu 1. The lag after the
in-game menu went, but the shelf and the menus got slower: on 0046 the
shelf drew at 30 fps, 297 missed latches in ten seconds before a game and
203 after one, against none on 7843ad0 this morning. Its sprites cost what
they did (6.5 ms a frame then, 6.1-8.4 now; per-pixel blits unchanged),
but its copy to the panel took 9.8 ms a frame against 2.7. Nothing in that
path changed between the two builds; the only thing that changed for the
thread running it is the CPU it was kept to.

Why cpu 0 is slower for it is not measured. On this kind of SoC cpu 0
commonly takes the interrupts, which would fit; that is a hypothesis.

The draw is free again. The emulator stays on cpu 1, which is what keeps
it from waking onto the draw's CPU after the menu, and the draw, free,
moves off whichever CPU the emulator is busy on. The spawned threads that
0045 unpinned only because the draw was pinned are back as they were;
`background()` still unpins, for the emulator's rewind worker.

With SLOT_TRACE_LATENCY the pace line also counts the frames the draw
spent on each CPU, so the next trace shows where it ran when it was fast
and when it was not.

Tests, native and as armv7 under qemu: a thread pinned to the emulator's
cpu reads that cpu back. slot (61), slot-power (5) and slot-gfx (47) lib
tests pass; clippy is clean on slot's lib and binary.

Applies on top of 0046, upstream 91499a9.

